IMPORT prelude
IMPORT daydate
IMPORT `il48-nouns`

-- Row il-48. THE CITATIONS, as typed values.
-- A citation is encoded as TWO descriptions: what the citing words say they point at, and what the target is. A test compares them. Where they differ, the citation does not point at what it says;
-- that is a defect, kept in an expected-red file with its count declared in check.sh and encoding.json.
-- Four citations are recorded, and one printed figure.
-- Two are defects: regulation 7 of the Youth and Apprentices regulations cites section 3(ג) of the Law, which was repealed on 1 April 1997; and the definition of "השכר הממוצע" in section 1 of the Law cites the 1968
-- consolidated version of the National Insurance Law, which the 1995 consolidated version replaced.
-- Two are sound and are here as controls: the same regulation's citation of section 3(ב)(3), and section 14א(א)'s citation of the three conditions in section 6א(א).
-- The printed figure is a control too: the divisor in the Law's definition of the hourly minimum wage, as the official instrument prints it and as the deposited consolidation prints it.
-- LIMBS. Limb (a) is the question whether the target is as the citing words say; limb (b) is the question whether the citing words still read so in the latest official text of the citing provision.
-- Each limb says where it was read from (see `Where a reading comes from` in il48-nouns.l4). The Hebrew Wikisource text is not official.
-- A date for which the sources held give no answer is NOTHING; there is no REFUSE.

§ `Cross-references`

GIVEN c IS A `A cross-reference`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`the citation points at what it says` MEANS c's `the citing words say it points at` EQUALS c's `the target is`

GIVEN c IS A `A cross-reference`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`both limbs were read from the official instruments` MEANS
    c's `limb (a) was read from` EQUALS `the official instrument, read from its page image`
    AND c's `limb (b) was read from` EQUALS `the official instrument, read from its page image`

-- Whether the text the citing words name is the text in force on a day. The only answer the sources held give is the day it stopped being in force and the days from then on; for any earlier day,
-- and for a citation with no recorded end, the answer is NOTHING (fork D1 in NOTES.md).
GIVEN c IS A `A cross-reference`
      day IS A DATE
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`the named text is in force on` MEANS
    CONSIDER c's `the text the citing words name stopped being the text in force on`
        WHEN JUST stopped THEN IF day AT LEAST stopped THEN JUST FALSE ELSE NOTHING
        WHEN NOTHING THEN NOTHING

-- The documented fork of the Interpretation Law section 25: whether the citing words, read through that section, reach the target. NOTHING where this row does not say.
GIVEN c IS A `A cross-reference`
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`the citation reaches its target once read through the Interpretation Law section 25` MEANS
    CONSIDER c's `read through the Interpretation Law section 25 the citing words reach`
        WHEN JUST reached THEN JUST (reached EQUALS c's `the target is`)
        WHEN NOTHING THEN NOTHING

